## Getting Started with clingo_elixir

### Introduction

This notebook introduces **the Elixir wrapper**: choosing an API, reading results,
and managing native state. To learn answer set programming itself, open the
[course contents](../README.md). Run this guide top to bottom in a local Livebook
runtime with Elixir 1.20 / OTP 29 and the build dependencies in the
[project README](../../README.md). Setup installs this checkout and Kino 0.19.0;
the first installation needs network access. These examples use local ASP text.

Clingo parses, grounds, and solves ASP. Elixir orchestrates that work and receives
copied values. There is no separate Elixir modeling language to learn.

### Solve an independent problem

Choose exactly one person to take a shift. Alice is unavailable.

```elixir
program = """
person(alice; bob; carol).
1 { assigned(P) : person(P) } 1.
:- assigned(alice).
#show assigned/1.
"""

result = Clingo.solve!(program, models: :all)
result
```

`Clingo.solve!/2` creates a control, adds the program, grounds it, solves it,
and closes the control. Use it for independent jobs. `models: :all` requests
all models; the default returns at most one. For files alone, use
`Clingo.solve!(files: [path], models: :all)`; no empty program string is needed.
You can also combine text and files with `Clingo.solve!(program, files: [path])`.
The [introductory lesson](../language/03-choices-and-constraints.livemd#choices-consequences-and-constraints)
shows both forms using a file created in the notebook.
`Clingo.models(files: [path], models: :all)` streams file-based results, and
`Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [path])` exports without searching.
Native solver settings can be passed
as `arguments: ["--seed=7"]`.

### Read results before presenting them

The result is a `%Clingo.Result{}`. Each `%Clingo.Model{}` contains copied shown
atom strings, optimization costs, and its own `optimality_proven` flag. Those
values remain usable after the control closes.

```elixir
:satisfiable = result.status
true = result.exhausted
[["assigned(bob)"], ["assigned(carol)"]] = Enum.sort(Enum.map(result.models, & &1.shown))
Course.display(result)
```

`Course.display/1` is a notebook presentation helper: it builds a Kino status
summary and model table. It does not solve again or change `result`.
`Course.table_data/1` provides ordinary row maps for your own table rendering.
Use the public `Clingo` API in application code; the course helper lives only
under `livebooks/support`.

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `status` | `:satisfiable`, `:unsatisfiable`, or `:unknown` |
| `exhausted` | Search finished, rather than stopping at a limit |
| `interrupted` | Search was interrupted |
| `model.cost` | Native optimization costs, in priority order |
| `model.optimality_proven` | Whether optimality was established for this model |

Finding a model does not establish optimality. An empty shown set is also a
valid model: it differs from finding no models.

```elixir
empty = Clingo.solve!("#show.", models: :all)
[%{shown: []}] = empty.models

unsat = Clingo.solve!("a. :- a.")
:unsatisfiable = unsat.status
[] = unsat.models
Kino.Layout.grid([Course.display(empty), Course.display(unsat)], columns: 1)
```

UNSAT is a successful solve outcome. Invalid ASP raises a `Clingo.Error` instead.

### Enumerate only what you need

```elixir
sample = Clingo.models("{a; b; c}.", models: :all) |> Enum.take(2)
2 = length(sample)
Enum.map(sample, & &1.shown)
```

`Clingo.models/2` is lazy. Each enumeration creates its own control and closes
it on completion, early halt, or an exception. `Enum.take/2` avoids collecting
all models. A list of sampled models does not include a final solve status;
use `solve!/2` when you need that result.

### Reuse a control within one workflow

An external atom lets an application change an input between solves without
rebuilding the grounded program.

```elixir
snapshots =
  Clingo.with_control(fn control ->
    :ok =
      Control.add!(control, "#external available. assigned(bob) :- available. #show assigned/1.")

    :ok = Control.ground!(control)
    before = Control.solve!(control)
    :ok = Control.assign_external!(control, "available", true)
    after_change = Control.solve!(control)
    %{before: before, after_change: after_change}
  end)

[%{shown: []}] = snapshots.before.models
[%{shown: ["assigned(bob)"]}] = snapshots.after_change.models
Course.display(snapshots.after_change)
```

`with_control` returns the callback's value directly and closes native state
when the callback finishes, including on exceptions. Mutating operations return
`:ok`; solves return copied results. `add!` supplies ASP source; `ground!`
instantiates rules before solving. Copied snapshots describe their original
solve and do not change when native state changes.

### Keep state across notebook cells deliberately

For exploration you can hold a control across cells. Here the next cell changes
an external input rather than adding the same program again.

```elixir
control = Control.new!()
:ok = Control.add!(control, "#external enabled. a :- enabled.")
:ok = Control.ground!(control)
Control.solve!(control) |> Course.display()
```

```elixir
:ok = Control.assign_external!(control, "enabled", true)
Control.solve!(control) |> Course.display()
```

Close the control before replacing it or finishing your notebook. Re-evaluating
a creation cell creates another control; it does not reset the previous one.
After closing, rerun from the creation cell to start a new workflow.

```elixir
:ok = Control.close(control)
:ok = Control.close(control)
```

Closing twice is harmless. Handles share mutable native state and become invalid
after close. Prefer `with_control` for complete jobs. Temporary grounding, solve
hook and copied-inspection storage is reclaimed after native completion. Escaped
views remain expired, and intentionally retained views keep their identities alive. See the [ownership and API guide](../../docs/ELIXIR_API.md).

### Handle a parsing failure

```elixir
try do
  Clingo.solve!("a(")
rescue
  error in Clingo.Error -> %{message: error.message, code: error.code}
end
```

Bang functions raise on failure. Catch errors when your application can act on
them; otherwise let the notebook stop at the failing cell. An ordinary UNSAT
result should be handled through `result.status`.
